{"title":"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 14","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Deriving_Formulas)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Deriving Formulas)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Elementary_Geometry)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Elementary Geometry)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Quick_n&#39;_Easy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Quick n' Easy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Simplest)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Simplest)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Law_of_Sines,_similar_to_Solution_5)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Law of Sines, similar to Solution 5)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Using_Law_of_Cosines)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Using Law of Cosines)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Regular octagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/5/055dcba798b1424c44c2606d913e0f2edab1c5ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDEFGH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"120\" height=\"14\" /> has area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> be the area of quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/7/577991bfcbdc9404a5871bae06764c4785c7048a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACEG$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" />. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/1/3a108eafd58599b4a23ff5dfbb6f9baaf357cbb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac{m}{n}?$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"24\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/1/971c2b501ee8cffa1d910d421f6655c61ed61082.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } \\frac{3}{4} \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5} \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"450\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/7/577991bfcbdc9404a5871bae06764c4785c7048a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACEG$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> is a square. WLOG <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/6/f56cbc52e5c7c0fab4ac50957077b6d18fb2ca57.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = 1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"65\" height=\"16\" /> then using Law of Cosines, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/5/115199b1bcbb30f74094ac15b18fe15c3e77a5d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC^2 = [ACEG] = 1^2 + 1^2 - 2 \\cos{135} = 2 + \\sqrt{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"378\" height=\"22\" /> The area of the octagon is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5e361354ca3682b6ea91651307bfdd02b59acd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ACEG]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" /> plus the area of the four congruent (by symmetry) isosceles triangles, all an angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/e/d8efc4595ab8d0d7d43d5ec1988b1b7dc8ecc7fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$135$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> in between two sides of length 1. Now, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/5/a35b1f440d58e48966f283860ba651bd584c1a35.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dfrac{m}{n} = \\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2} + 4 \\cdot \\tfrac{1}{2} \\sin{135}} = \\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{2 + 2 \\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\]\" width=\"361\" height=\"49\" /> The answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/d/3bd9d1003fa0ecb744b1ebc09b28550c6bcf3372.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"43\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Refer to the diagram. Call one of the side lengths of the square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. Since quadrilateral ACEG is a square, the area of the square would just be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/2/752ccf51ef0be42644a0099346e876310df962f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"17\" />, which we can find by applying Law of Cosines on one of the four triangles. Assume each of the sides of the octagon has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Since each angle measures <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b54930ee3937d1087e4262a992f6770c261947e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$135^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> in an octagon, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/e/62ede29a704e417a7b770fb71f1c46b3343c53ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s^2 = 1^2+1^2-2\\cos(135^{\\circ}) = 2+\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"285\" height=\"24\" /> \n</p><p>There are many ways to find the area of the octagon, but one way is to split the octagon into two trapezoids and one rectangle. We can easily compute AF to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/8/9c85140b66951d85558fc8f18c2bd1c8eacd221f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> from splitting one of the sides into two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/2/22231a5cdd4fe05a11f4b577e3b81221f3017a85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45-45-90$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"98\" height=\"13\" /> triangles. So the area of the octagon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/2/232a76a12bc26c38cf99a52e39cc1cf045982b92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\cdot\\frac{1+\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1+\\sqrt{2} \\Rightarrow 2+2\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"254\" height=\"43\" />. \n</p><p>Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1330c1d39b51d859f54b4e08bcb24a19f88cf1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{m}{n} = \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+2\\sqrt{2}}\\]\" width=\"109\" height=\"45\" /> \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/5/435f7caa420f82736a80ed942abb58a25165f0be.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+2\\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{2-2\\sqrt{2}}{2-2\\sqrt{2}}\\]\" width=\"171\" height=\"45\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/e/2ee3ed8e82a1c3e4cc960ba975dcbb2dcf9748cb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow \\frac{-2\\sqrt{2}}{-4}\\]\" width=\"72\" height=\"41\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/6/55649124ce0b0bcde834425cb13a058d6d926350.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\Rightarrow \\boxed{\\textbf{(B)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\\]\" width=\"91\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>~Solution by IronicNinja ~Edited by sl_hc\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Deriving_Formulas.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Deriving_Formulas)\">Solution 3 (Deriving Formulas)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Deriving Formulas)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/5/655fec3cfa04ea44c5e8b5cfbff9649333ca8159.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((1, 2.41421356)--(2.41421356, 1)--(2.41421356, -1)--(1, -2.41421356)--(-1, -2.41421356)--(-2.41421356, -1)--(-2.41421356, 1)--(-1, 2.41421356)--cycle); draw((2.41421356, 1)--(1, -2.41421356)--(-2.41421356, -1)--(-1, 2.41421356)--cycle); label(&quot;A&quot;,(-1, 2.41421356),NW); label(&quot;B&quot;,(1, 2.41421356),NE); label(&quot;C&quot;,(2.41421356, 1),NE); label(&quot;D&quot;,(2.41421356, -1),SE); label(&quot;E&quot;,(1,-2.41421356),SE); label(&quot;F&quot;,(-1,-2.41421356),SW); label(&quot;G&quot;,(-2.41421356,-1),SW); label(&quot;H&quot;,(-2.41421356,1),NW); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>The first thing to notice is that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/7/577991bfcbdc9404a5871bae06764c4785c7048a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACEG$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> is a square. This is because, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/8/4588a93de6392dce84d356d07af69da8c0eb8f75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\bigtriangleup ABC \\cong \\bigtriangleup CDE \\cong \\bigtriangleup EFG \\cong \\bigtriangleup GHE$\" width=\"312\" height=\"13\" />, they all have the same base, meaning that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/a/92a9d07b4373b0d2040211d7976d94c4a6ef5945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC = DE = EG = GA$\" width=\"187\" height=\"13\" />. Hence, we have that it is a square. To determine the area of this square, we can determine the length of its diagonals.\n</p><p>In order to do this, we first determine the area of the octagon. Letting the side length be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, we can create a square of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> around it (see figure).\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/4/d745144354c57482a7b31f007bf39ad9862254be.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((1, 2.41421356)--(2.41421356, 1)--(2.41421356, -1)--(1, -2.41421356)--(-1, -2.41421356)--(-2.41421356, -1)--(-2.41421356, 1)--(-1, 2.41421356)--cycle); label(&quot;a&quot;,(1, 2.41421356)--(2.41421356, 1),S); draw((-2.41421356, 2.41421356)--(2.41421356, 2.41421356)--(2.41421356, -2.41421356)--(-2.41421356, -2.41421356)--cycle); label(&quot;s&quot;,(-2.41421356, 2.41421356)--(2.41421356, 2.41421356),N); [/asy]\" width=\"235\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p>Creating a small square of side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> from the corners of this figure gives us an area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/9/9698c992334be77221be8c8c819909eca34fde9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^2$\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/1/9d113a9b13939ccfb2def85bda16b1420e57f21c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s^2 - a^2 = n$\" width=\"90\" height=\"15\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is the area of the octagon. We know from the Pythagorean Theorem that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/9/27937ebf0d25dbdbda69e11980af278315bf8e06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s = a + \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"91\" height=\"37\" />, meaning that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/7/987eb96d600200dc309e67488356e3ab6a988832.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = (a + \\frac{a}{\\sqrt{2}})^2 - a^2 = 2a^2(1+\\sqrt{2})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"274\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>Dividing this by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> gives us the area of each triangular segment which makes up the octagon. Further dividing by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> gives us the area of a smaller segment consisting of the right triangle with legs of the apothem and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/f/9afddc3c61aa4e66d003d0c7cc5bd01560d341f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"34\" />. Using the area of a triangle as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/d/ddd1952ffb713991003b4c82e0150177396b8271.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}bh$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"31\" height=\"37\" />, we can determine the length of apothem <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> from\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/9/589c5dc48117bef7e3af4627d00178879c3f9cf3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2a^2(1+\\sqrt{2})}{8 \\times 2} = \\frac{\\frac{a}{2}\\times r}{2}\\]\" width=\"166\" height=\"41\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/b/44be5281f687c3d155cdb8b0816593dbc67b1c14.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{4a^2(1+\\sqrt{2})}{8 } = ar\\]\" width=\"140\" height=\"41\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/9/9c90842411d8b8d2006c0a03bf5177dc5d4a436e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{a(1+\\sqrt{2})}{2} = r\\]\" width=\"113\" height=\"41\" />.\n</p><p>From the apothem, we can once again use the Pythagorean Theorem, giving us the length of the circumradius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/3/fd3f44a7dc276d8663d948d9a2c2f5f0a304591a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R^2 = (\\frac{a(1+\\sqrt{2})}{2})^2 + (\\frac{a}{2})^2\\]\" width=\"206\" height=\"41\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591d73087660900098fce4415f3b28414cd23c6f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R^2 = \\frac{a^2(1+\\sqrt{2})^2}{4} + \\frac{a^2}{4}\\]\" width=\"185\" height=\"41\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/d/efdbfcfc65a3590616e04326863ccfe0c0b81138.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R^2 = \\frac{a^2(3+2\\sqrt{2})}{4} + \\frac{a^2}{4}\\]\" width=\"187\" height=\"41\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/6/c6605a53da9a5b31b1236d6694ec43121c9ab160.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R^2 = \\frac{a^2(4+2\\sqrt{2})}{4}\\]\" width=\"143\" height=\"41\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/7/d87899ba8dd53e0df69a41cbd2b3578a0b669145.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R = \\sqrt{\\frac{a^2(4+2\\sqrt{2})}{4}} = \\frac{a\\sqrt{4 + 2\\sqrt2}}{2}\\]\" width=\"274\" height=\"53\" />.\n</p><p>Doubling this gives us the diagonal of both the square and the octagon. From here, we can use the formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/c/99c65c4e2db64d5667be65b0fd9e60b304e3b10e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = \\frac{1}{2}d^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"66\" height=\"37\" /> for the area of the square:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/a/91aed20e1bdc2cca1dca304cabeebd39bc5979f9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = \\frac{(a\\sqrt{4 + 2\\sqrt2})^2}{2}\\]\" width=\"153\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/5/9653cfe1c579e34274b508ee9890ec2302dbb265.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A = \\frac{a^2(4+2\\sqrt{2})}{2} = m\\]\" width=\"177\" height=\"41\" />.\n</p><p>Thus we now only need to find the ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27f583697295fc6f723862c13228ee55107c33ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"22\" height=\"34\" />. This can be easily done through some algebra:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/1/a1167218d964f084ef68fe2d9de7c1ac63e40e8d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{m}{n} = \\frac{a^2(4+2\\sqrt{2})}{2(2a^2(1+\\sqrt{2})}\\]\" width=\"156\" height=\"47\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/4/774d7cd9807843803b12eb2319b6058e37bac1b8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{m}{n} = \\frac{4+2\\sqrt{2}}{4+4\\sqrt{2}}\\]\" width=\"109\" height=\"45\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1330c1d39b51d859f54b4e08bcb24a19f88cf1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{m}{n} = \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+2\\sqrt{2}}\\]\" width=\"109\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Rationalizing the denominator by multiplying by the conjugate, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/4/f846b23bd3f1eaea1ceb50b559adcc760f2e1a1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"119\" height=\"53\" />. ~ciceronii\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/2/36267c5f4ee67ae71ac26988998233a17818f69b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Note:}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> this can more easily be done if you know any of these formulas. This was an entire derivation of the area of an octagon, it's apothem, and it's circumradius, but it can be much simpler if you have any of these memorized.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/5/5d5e8916ef049c05fedb3773380918c90c94a5b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Note No.2:}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"94\" height=\"13\" /> The formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/4/0a4e9112804867f39afbb4c0afcff248b4d585f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[ABCDEFG]=(\\sqrt{2})[ACEG]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"243\" height=\"24\" /> is one from the Intro to Geometry Textbook: Problem 16.46.</li></ul>\n<p>~Note No.2 by athreyay\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/0/a504fb200319a88926a9d442546df4067ccdf6ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{Note No.3:}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"94\" height=\"13\" /> after trying my self, setting the sides as 1 would be much easier to solve as numbers eaisly cancel out</li></ul>\n<p>~Note~ chundoowan\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Elementary_Geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Elementary_Geometry)\">Solution 3 (Elementary Geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Elementary Geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>WLOG, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/0/24096979a27c72ccde79e7e4889bf4e4be7a632b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />. Let the intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/d/c9daba06f5ec9c5069f24bbeab9c7151ab4f68c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/2/de2e9f77cf5375d42ace718f4059234d9eb51230.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$GD$\" width=\"29\" height=\"12\" /> be point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/4/934559a7690c08490b9fb854e0435eb6766802e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$GIF$\" width=\"37\" height=\"12\" /> is an isosceles right triangle (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/9/689fafa30cf28ecc5856cd91ab83df9ae9bf24c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\angle HGF=135^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"131\" height=\"13\" />), so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/4/da4f66c5884ec21113e4a73d703f96a4eb2df82d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IG=IF=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"122\" height=\"41\" />. The distance between each side and the center is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/4/c149223baaef55a72e4b3a639cd5b11a1e76e321.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IF+\\frac{1}{2}GH=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"176\" height=\"41\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/5/055dcba798b1424c44c2606d913e0f2edab1c5ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCDEFGH$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"120\" height=\"14\" /> is 8 triangles of base 1 and altitude <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/9/ca90cf99a10da34dd5589e1657b6046a36127dc2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"62\" height=\"41\" />, so its area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/a/a5a05001ae6863e364704e521645fa3b46e122c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8(\\frac{1}{2})(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"116\" height=\"43\" /> or \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/c/21ce39942e219ccf9e56b29995ddcfd3dbdacb80.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2+2\\sqrt{2}\\]\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" />\nSimilarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/7/577991bfcbdc9404a5871bae06764c4785c7048a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ACEG$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> is clearly a  square of area <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/3/003cc431bc18be95b53462dd1a0fe9555ed1aed9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$GA^2$\" width=\"34\" height=\"15\" />. By the Pythagorean Theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/4/a24dd3920fd7b45d20f3de95586dc16e14b8d858.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$GA^2=GI^2+IA^2=(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^2+(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"323\" height=\"43\" /> or \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/0/2b0c63045508d7617416a7442fec358919b5d276.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2+\\sqrt{2}\\]\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f1330c1d39b51d859f54b4e08bcb24a19f88cf1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{m}{n} = \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+2\\sqrt{2}}\\]\" width=\"109\" height=\"45\" />\nFrom some fast manipulations, see that it is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/6/0067eff825ecb52c099ca3ab84b1805ac795efa0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{B)}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{2}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"61\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>~~BJHHar\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Quick_n.27_Easy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Quick_n'_Easy)\">Solution 4 (Quick n' Easy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Quick n&#39; Easy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This solution is quite efficient and unconvoluted. Though the explanation appears long, this is only because I have tried to make it very thorough. \n</p><p>Because we are finding the ratios of the areas of the quadrilateral, and the octagon, let us assume, for simplicity's sake, that the octagon has side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Call <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> the center of the octagon. Because it is a regular octagon, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/6/f76e209ba6efb92105415d4f3c62ef804a52519d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AOB$\" width=\"55\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6656c410b8645473cf8e5b7be7633dcee4296b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{360}{8}=45$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"76\" height=\"38\" />. \nDraw the apothem, and call where it intersects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. As the apothem bisects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/6/f76e209ba6efb92105415d4f3c62ef804a52519d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AOB$\" width=\"55\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/e/f7e789e545828ee200438ba59b8813a25aad146c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AIB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/f/b9f0310385d98baa48ebdb622e4b8ac5ac4b596b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{45}{2}=22.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />. \nThen, as the apothem is perpendicular to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/4/f044218fce18b8e29d2693c3b0677845d8e18529.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle IBO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/5/2b5c62262fb7e6d729e4206bd743a62029f1c320.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90-22.5=67.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"131\" height=\"15\" />. As the apothem bisects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/0/d901aec126c5c3fa9c5d5866c2d06a1486989f2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$IB$\" width=\"23\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Thus, the apothem has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/b/4ab38ed8afc2d0d88e5b8500c2d6c53203d52ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{tan}(67.5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"98\" height=\"38\" />. By the formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/7/647d1af67e314ced7727727f8f61c6bb6f8dd5f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{area} = \\text{apothem} \\cdot \\frac{\\text{perimeter}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"207\" height=\"38\" />, the area of the octagon is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/1/6812ca783f2d5a14e3554520b39af484fec025c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{tan}(67.5) \\cdot \\frac{8}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"125\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Now, we move on to the quadrilateral. Let us take triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/9/61986fb9aca15a3d9fa47a37f10e6cc396e2ed1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AGH$\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" />. By the interior angle formula, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/0/a500f4c0318d6cc9ff35de72de4b92a2cdaabc71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\angle AGH = \\frac{180 (8-2)}{8} = 135$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"237\" height=\"40\" />. Because the octagon is regular, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/9/61986fb9aca15a3d9fa47a37f10e6cc396e2ed1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AGH$\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" /> is isosceles, with assumed sidelength <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Drop an altitude from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b11fca7fa0396390456782405b2897976a413f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, and call the intersection point, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. As <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/9/61986fb9aca15a3d9fa47a37f10e6cc396e2ed1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AGH$\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" /> is isosceles, this altitude bisects both segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b11fca7fa0396390456782405b2897976a413f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/0/44025d179650d65e4da46151a9dec32db4423c89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AHG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/6/436c90bd0d763c585c30673544749035ecb6e42f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\angle AHP = \\frac{135}{2} = 67.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"187\" height=\"38\" />. Let us call the length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b11fca7fa0396390456782405b2897976a413f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, which is also a side of the quadrilateral, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3bb4eeb347e74e025fd175c22f1f7275af0555e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/3/c5372e4a6aa030c5c2973cc7aad79bad38f9241b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{s}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"33\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/3/1b3c6f5610c5e9ae4e8e5761c00133df29d528f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{sin}(67.5) = \\frac{\\frac{s}{2}}{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"108\" height=\"40\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/2/8f2ea291642e046334f0b75f0d966d34da6974d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s = 2 \\cdot \\text{sin}(67.5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"124\" height=\"20\" />. We can do the same for each of the other sides of the quadrilateral and find that they are of the same length. One can easily see that triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> is congruent to triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/9/61986fb9aca15a3d9fa47a37f10e6cc396e2ed1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AGH$\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" />. Because triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/9/61986fb9aca15a3d9fa47a37f10e6cc396e2ed1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AGH$\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" /> is isosceles, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/0/7b059304a6b88087afa395a60b0fe598b3e058ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\angle HAG = \\frac{180-135}{2} = 22.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"237\" height=\"38\" />. The same can be said of angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/d/2edcdc0d73ef64ec441f60a3a8dccdbd10aa61a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BAC$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" />. Thus, angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/a/0ea1f1b90e590e8768d6b5ff80702b7908d90b2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CAG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />, an interior angle of the quadrilateral is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/c/bfcfee7bd8a5e15e5f6b7881c138de43651572a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. We can do the same for the other interior angles of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/1/981d0222fe1de2469212d646e97a70a0ea39699e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CEGA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> and find that they are also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/c/bfcfee7bd8a5e15e5f6b7881c138de43651572a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/1/981d0222fe1de2469212d646e97a70a0ea39699e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CEGA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> is a square, so its area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/d/46d284d0fe7269d73f557f1cd8d94d129c9b03e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s^2 = 4 \\cdot \\text{sin}^2(67.5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"139\" height=\"21\" />. \n</p><p>Now, we must find the ratios of the two areas.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/4/c240dd430a07b2f5bae038768387f9736193b813.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\text{Area(quadrilateral)}}{\\text{Area(hexagon)}}  = 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\text{sin}^2(67.5)}{2\\cdot \\text{tan}(67.5)}  =frac{2\\cdot \\text{sin}^2(67.5)}{\\frac{\\text{sin}(67.5)}{\\text{cos}(67.5)}}   = 2\\cdot \\text{sin}(67.5) \\cdot \\text{cos}(67.5)   = \\text{sin} (135) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"853\" height=\"48\" />, the answer.\n</p><p>-jyatvitskiy&#160;:)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Simplest.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Simplest)\">Solution 5 (Simplest)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Simplest)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/2/5a21a7b0de44b4ddb0e53cfd0d4e53aa895c661f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] draw((1, 2.41421356)--(2.41421356, 1)--(2.41421356, -1)--(1, -2.41421356)--(-1, -2.41421356)--(-2.41421356, -1)--(-2.41421356, 1)--(-1, 2.41421356)--cycle); draw((2.41421356, 1)--(1, -2.41421356)--(-2.41421356, -1)--(-1, 2.41421356)--cycle); draw((-1, 2.41421356)--(1,-2.41421356)); draw((1, 2.41421356)--(-1,-2.41421356)); draw((2.41421356, 1)--(-2.41421356,-1)); draw((2.41421356, -1)--(-2.41421356,1)); label(&quot;A&quot;,(-1, 2.41421356),NW); label(&quot;B&quot;,(1, 2.41421356),NE); label(&quot;C&quot;,(2.41421356, 1),NE); label(&quot;D&quot;,(2.41421356, -1),SE); label(&quot;E&quot;,(1,-2.41421356),SE); label(&quot;F&quot;,(-1,-2.41421356),SW); label(&quot;G&quot;,(-2.41421356,-1),SW); label(&quot;H&quot;,(-2.41421356,1),NW); label(&quot;O&quot;,(.1,.1),NE); label(&quot;P&quot;,(.60710678,1.60710678),SE);  [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p>The ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/d/5fdd688b5ae56da028f408e05da1bdaa25ccb1bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n}=\\frac{[OPC]}{[OBC]}=\\frac{OP}{OB}=\\frac{OP}{OC}=\\frac{\\sqrt {2}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"266\" height=\"46\" />.\n</p><p>&lt;&lt;ERMS Coach&gt;&gt;\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Law_of_Sines.2C_similar_to_Solution_5.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Law_of_Sines,_similar_to_Solution_5)\">Solution 6 (Law of Sines, similar to Solution 5)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Law of Sines, similar to Solution 5)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the center of the octagon. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c53cc93aa43cc55bea0dc2511833fe6bdda020f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AO$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/d/eadb64f51f3d2f727a5e340539621cac9a23087e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$HB$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" /> intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/f/87fa832bf21ebc48a46289cd7aea73ffcc84c111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X.$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> By symmetry, the problem reduces to finding the ratio between the area of the triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/0/890bd5d40a42153edcb992ad0a14cac52ca2eaf8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABO$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/a/93a3283252128b249659f8ad42fedeeda34aa835.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OXB,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" /> which is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/f/46fa6be384ec513b9c1324406de2515ac81d6049.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}XO\\cdot BO \\sin{\\theta}}{\\frac{1}{2}AO \\cdot BO \\sin{\\theta}} = \\frac{XO}{AO} = \\frac{XO}{BO} = \\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"314\" height=\"55\" /> Here, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e8ed7a3ba22130ad3984eb2cd413406475a689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\theta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> represents the angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/d/4dda9e3886fd325e7da13369716757ce25c5e88a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AOB.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_.28Using_Law_of_Cosines.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn_(Using_Law_of_Cosines)\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Using Law of Cosines)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn (Using Law of Cosines)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/4_x1sgcQCp4?t=5605\">https://youtu.be/4_x1sgcQCp4?t=5605</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A\">2020 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 13\">Problem 13</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 15\">Problem 15</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_4\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_5\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_7\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_8\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_16\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_18\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_19\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_20\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_12_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 12 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 12 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017030002\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.072 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.209 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 815/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 2298/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 158/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 27773/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   14.060      1 -total\n 60.93%    8.567      1 Template:AMC12_box\n 37.13%    5.221      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:18137:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017030002 and revision id 288302. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}